# Base-Screen Focus Pollution Warnings

Status: proposed; revised after Busted lifecycle analysis

## Purpose

Warn when an example or suite leaves Dwarf Fortress on a different base-game
viewscreen or base-game focus than the state it inherited.

The warnings are nonfatal test-hygiene diagnostics. They expose both:

- inter-test pollution caused by per-example setup, the example body, or
  per-example teardown; and
- inter-suite pollution caused by suite setup, examples, or suite teardown.

DwarfSpec-owned components must be removed before the relevant final
observation. Their temporary DFHack screens do not count as base-game focus
changes.

## Terminology

- This plan maps the requested `lazy_begin` boundary to Busted's
  `lazy_setup` lifecycle.
- A suite is every executable Busted context that can own `lazy_setup` and
  `lazy_teardown`, including nested `describe` contexts and the outer repeat
  context.
- An example is one Busted `it`, `test`, or `spec` case.
- A base-game screen is the topmost non-DFHack viewscreen returned by
  `dfhack.gui.getDFViewscreen(true)`.
- A change warning is a detached `diagnostic.recorded` event whose content has
  warning severity. There is no separate warning-event type.

## State model

Each active suite owns three observations:

- `S0`, suite entry: captured immediately before that suite's own
  `lazy_setup` begins, after ancestor suites have established their state.
- `S1`, suite prepared: captured immediately after that suite's
  `lazy_setup` completes.
- `S2`, suite exit: captured after that suite's `lazy_teardown`, DwarfSpec
  cleanup, automatic unmounting, and cleanup settlement complete.

Each example owns two observations:

- `T0`, example entry: captured after active suite setup and DwarfSpec's
  pre-example reset, but before project `before_each` hooks.
- `T1`, example exit: captured after Busted has published the example result,
  project `after_each` hooks have run, and DwarfSpec cleanup, automatic
  unmounting, and cleanup settlement complete.

The comparisons are:

- example pollution: compare `T1` with `T0`;
- suite pollution: compare `S2` with `S0`.

`S1` is not compared with `S0` to produce an immediate warning. Suite setup is
allowed to establish a temporary working screen. `S1` instead:

- becomes the prepared context from which descendant examples run;
- distinguishes setup state that was never restored from a later unrelated
  change; and
- is included in suite-warning diagnostics.

Nested suites use a stack. A child suite's `S0` is captured after its ancestors'
`lazy_setup` hooks have run and before its own `lazy_setup`. The child is
compared against that inherited state, not against the run's original screen.

## Observation and comparison contract

Each observation has two strictly separated parts:

- a private in-process comparison token containing the exact viewscreen
  userdata identity; and
- a detached diagnostic record containing only bounded JSON-safe data.

The private identity:

- exists only until its example or suite comparison completes;
- is used only for exact equality comparison; and
- must never enter an event, log line, result, failure, snapshot, or public
  test API.

The detached observation contains:

- screen availability;
- a stable screen type label without an address;
- focus availability;
- a defensive copy of the focus strings;
- a canonical focus set used for comparison; and
- bounded error text when a field is unavailable.

Comparison produces independent statuses:

- `screen_comparison`: `same`, `changed`, or `unavailable`;
- `focus_comparison`: `same`, `changed`, or `unavailable`;
- `details_complete`: whether both dimensions were comparable.

Focus comparison uses canonical set membership. Incidental ordering of
equivalent focus strings does not count as a change.

A change is affirmatively known when:

- both private screen identities are available and differ; or
- both canonical focus sets are available and differ.

When either dimension proves a change, emit one change warning even if the
other dimension is unavailable. The warning must contain every available
before/after detail and explicitly mark incomplete focus or screen evidence.

If neither dimension proves a change but one or both dimensions are
unavailable, publish a non-warning
`base_screen_focus_verification_incomplete` diagnostic. Do not falsely claim
that the state changed.

If both dimensions are available and unchanged, publish nothing.

## Warning scopes and attribution

Example warnings use:

- `scope='example'`;
- the retained example name and source identity when Busted published
  `test.started`; and
- the containing suite path.

Busted publishes `test.finished` before any `after_each` hook. Therefore:

- example warnings appear after `SUCCESS`, `FAILURE`, or `ERROR` result output;
- `run.current_test` has already been cleared when comparison occurs; and
- DwarfSpec must retain a separate lifecycle identity until its post-example
  hook consumes it.

If `before_each` fails before Busted publishes `test.started`, comparison still
runs. Attribute any warning to the containing suite and identify it as an
example-setup failure without inventing an example name.

Suite warnings use:

- `scope='suite'`;
- a stable full suite path and source identity;
- `S0`, `S1`, and `S2`; and
- the active repeat index.

An example leak that remains through suite exit may produce both an example
warning and a suite warning. This is intentional: the records prove pollution
at two different ownership boundaries.

## Cleanup and mount exclusions

- `T0` is captured only after `reset('before example')` succeeds.
- `T1` is captured only after project `after_each`, successful
  `reset('after example')`, and its existing one-frame settlement wait.
- `S2` is captured only after project `lazy_teardown`, suite-bound DwarfSpec
  cleanup, automatic unmounting, and settlement.
- Suite entry is not normalized or restored before `S0`; inherited pollution
  is the state the suite actually received.
- `S1` may be observed while suite-created DFHack screens exist, but
  `getDFViewscreen(true)` resolves beneath them to the base-game screen.
- Ordinary widgets, overlay widgets, complete `gui.ZScreen` instances, and
  non-owning native attachments do not count merely because they were mounted.
- Input performed through a mount still counts when it changes the underlying
  base-game screen or focus and leaves that change behind.
- If DwarfSpec reset or settlement fails, preserve the cleanup error as the
  authoritative error, clear guard state, and skip the affected final
  comparison. Do not emit a misleading verification or change warning.
- Emergency abort and generation-loss paths clear unconsumed guard state and
  rely on the existing run cleanup report.

DwarfSpec reports pollution but does not navigate or restore arbitrary
base-game UI state automatically.

## Diagnostic record

Use the existing `diagnostic.recorded` event. Extend its accepted content
directly; DwarfSpec has not released this event contract and requires no
backward-compatibility path or schema-version migration.

A change diagnostic has:

```lua
{
    kind='base_screen_focus_changed',
    content={
        severity='warning',
        scope='example' or 'suite',
        owner_name='stable example or suite name',
        source_identity='project-relative source when available',
        repeat_index=1,
        screen_comparison='same' or 'changed' or 'unavailable',
        focus_comparison='same' or 'changed' or 'unavailable',
        details_complete=true or false,
        before={...},
        prepared={...}, -- suite scope only
        after={...},
    },
}
```

Only detached diagnostic content is communicated. The exact private screen
identity is represented solely by the computed comparison status.

Append one stable `WARNING ...` line to retained run logs from the same
diagnostic record. Format warning-severity diagnostics in live CLI output.
Do not add them to `failure_details`.

Warnings and incomplete-verification diagnostics must not change:

- Busted success, failure, error, or pending counts;
- terminal run state;
- process exit status;
- `cleanup_confirmed`;
- mount-cleanup verification; or
- scheduler ownership and recovery behavior.

## Non-goals

- Do not add automatic focus restoration.
- Do not add a warning allowlist, suppression command, or configuration option
  until observed usage demonstrates a need.
- Do not treat DFHack-owned screens as base-game viewscreens.
- Do not broaden the guard to pointer state, map position, selection, pause
  state, simulation state, or arbitrary game data.
- Do not change the thin `tools/Run-AutomationTests.ps1` wrapper.
- Do not expose a public API for the guard's private observations.

## Phase 1: Isolate base-screen observation and comparison

- [ ] Add
  `src/dwarfspec/automation/base_screen_focus_guard.lua` with injected GUI
  dependencies.
- [ ] Capture the base screen with `dfhack.gui.getDFViewscreen(true)`.
- [ ] Store exact screen identity only in the private comparison token.
- [ ] Produce bounded detached screen and focus diagnostics.
- [ ] Copy and canonicalize focus strings without retaining or modifying
  DFHack-owned tables.
- [ ] Return explicit screen, focus, and completeness comparison statuses.
- [ ] Produce a change record when either comparable dimension changed.
- [ ] Preserve affirmative screen-change evidence when focus details are
  incomplete.
- [ ] Produce a non-warning incomplete-verification record only when no change
  is affirmatively known.
- [ ] Add LuaDoc comments to every new or changed function, class, and
  class-like record.

### Phase 1 tests

- [ ] Add `tests/unit/base_screen_focus_guard_spec.lua`.
- [ ] Prove identical identity and focus produce no diagnostic.
- [ ] Prove a different screen instance with the same type and focus is a
  screen change.
- [ ] Prove a changed focus set on the same screen is a focus change.
- [ ] Prove focus ordering alone is ignored.
- [ ] Prove duplicate focus strings do not alter set comparison.
- [ ] Prove a known screen change plus unavailable focus produces one change
  warning with incomplete focus details.
- [ ] Prove a known focus change plus unavailable screen identity produces one
  change warning with incomplete screen details.
- [ ] Prove entirely unavailable comparison produces an incomplete-
  verification diagnostic instead of a false change warning.
- [ ] Prove diagnostic records contain no userdata, mutable source tables, or
  address-bearing labels.
- [ ] Prove error strings, focus counts, and focus-string lengths are bounded.

### Phase 1 gate

- [ ] Focused guard unit tests pass.
- [ ] Full unit suite passes.
- [ ] Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.

## Phase 2: Add a pinned Busted suite-lifecycle adapter

- [ ] Add
  `src/dwarfspec/automation/busted_lifecycle_adapter.lua` as the only module
  permitted to depend on Busted 2.3.0 context and hook-registration details.
- [ ] Map the requested suite lifecycle to Busted `lazy_setup` and
  `lazy_teardown`.
- [ ] Instrument every suite-producing context and alias used by the embedded
  Busted host, including nested suites.
- [ ] Inject a suite-entry callback before the suite's first project
  `lazy_setup` callback.
- [ ] Inject a suite-prepared callback after the suite's final project
  `lazy_setup` callback.
- [ ] Inject a suite-exit callback after the suite's final project
  `lazy_teardown` callback.
- [ ] Preserve project hook order and do not expose internal hooks in the
  project test environment.
- [ ] Ensure ancestor suite setup completes before a child suite captures its
  entry state.
- [ ] Ensure a child suite exits before its parent suite teardown and final
  comparison.
- [ ] Instrument suites with no project lazy hooks so entry and prepared
  observations are still well-defined.
- [ ] Reinstall lifecycle instrumentation when Busted rebuilds contexts for
  `repeat_count > 1`; do not rely on one post-discovery tree traversal.
- [ ] Provide stable suite paths and source identities without retaining the
  complete Busted context after suite exit.
- [ ] Keep ordinary strict setup/teardown behavior unchanged and document its
  ordering relative to the lazy suite boundary.

### Phase 2 tests

- [ ] Add `tests/unit/busted_lifecycle_adapter_spec.lua` using the pinned real
  Busted runtime.
- [ ] Prove root, file-level, and nested suite callback order.
- [ ] Prove multiple project lazy hooks retain their declared order between
  DwarfSpec's entry and prepared callbacks.
- [ ] Prove project lazy teardown completes before DwarfSpec's exit callback.
- [ ] Prove parent setup, child entry/setup/examples/teardown/exit, and parent
  exit form a correctly nested stack.
- [ ] Prove suites without project lazy hooks receive adjacent entry and
  prepared callbacks.
- [ ] Prove lazy-setup failure still permits safe suite teardown and guard-state
  release.
- [ ] Prove lazy-teardown failure does not prevent the final DwarfSpec callback
  when Busted continues hook execution.
- [ ] Prove two or more Busted repeats receive independent suite records with
  no stale identities.
- [ ] Prove pending and filtered examples do not create phantom active suite or
  example records.

### Phase 2 gate

- [ ] Focused lifecycle-adapter unit tests pass.
- [ ] Existing Busted discovery, filtering, and repeat-count tests pass.
- [ ] Full unit suite passes.
- [ ] Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.

## Phase 3: Integrate per-example pollution detection

- [ ] Split the current combined lifecycle installation in
  `src/dwarfspec/automation/host.lua`.
- [ ] Register the internal pre-example callback before project specs execute.
- [ ] Run `reset('before example')` before capturing `T0`.
- [ ] Register the internal post-example callback after project hooks so every
  project `after_each` runs first.
- [ ] Run `reset('after example')` and its settlement wait before capturing
  `T1`.
- [ ] Compare `T1` with `T0` and publish any diagnostic after Busted's
  `test.finished` event.
- [ ] Retain example attribution independently from `run.current_test` until
  post-example comparison completes.
- [ ] Clear attribution before the next example and on every failure or abort
  path.
- [ ] Attribute pre-`test.started` setup failures to the containing suite
  without reusing a previous example name.
- [ ] Preserve existing cleanup errors without adding a comparison after failed
  reset or settlement.

### Phase 3 tests

- [ ] Extend `tests/unit/automation_host_spec.lua` for the actual order:
  reset, `T0`, project `before_each`, test result, project `after_each`, reset,
  settlement, `T1`, diagnostic.
- [ ] Prove warning output follows `SUCCESS`, `FAILURE`, or `ERROR`.
- [ ] Prove top-level and nested project `after_each` hooks run before `T1`.
- [ ] Prove example failure does not prevent cleanup and comparison.
- [ ] Prove project `after_each` failure does not prevent the final internal
  hook when Busted continues.
- [ ] Prove `before_each` failure cannot inherit the preceding example name.
- [ ] Prove automatic cleanup for every mount category completes before `T1`.
- [ ] Prove reset failure remains authoritative and skips comparison.
- [ ] Prove an example warning leaves counts, terminal state, exit status, and
  cleanup confirmation unchanged.

### Phase 3 gate

- [ ] Focused host lifecycle tests pass.
- [ ] Existing combined assertion/cleanup, timeout, abort, and mount-recovery
  fixtures remain green.
- [ ] Full unit suite passes.
- [ ] Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.

## Phase 4: Integrate inter-suite pollution detection

- [ ] Create one active suite record on entry and push it onto the nested
  lifecycle stack.
- [ ] Capture `S0` before the suite's own lazy setup.
- [ ] Capture `S1` after its lazy setup.
- [ ] Use the active prepared suite state as context for descendant examples
  without replacing their independent `T0` observations.
- [ ] After project lazy teardown, drain suite-exit DwarfSpec cleanup and wait
  for settlement before capturing `S2`.
- [ ] Compare `S2` with `S0`; include `S1` in any suite change diagnostic.
- [ ] Pop and release the complete suite record after comparison.
- [ ] Allow both example and suite warnings when pollution crosses both
  boundaries.
- [ ] Keep sibling suite baselines independent so the next suite records the
  state it actually inherited.
- [ ] Handle suites whose setup fails, whose teardown fails, or which contain
  no runnable examples without leaking guard records.
- [ ] Clear the entire suite stack during emergency abort and generation loss.

### Phase 4 tests

- [ ] Prove `S0 == S2` emits no suite warning even when `S1` differs.
- [ ] Prove `S0 != S1` and `S1 == S2` reports setup state that was never
  restored.
- [ ] Prove `S2` differing from both `S0` and `S1` retains all three states in
  the diagnostic.
- [ ] Prove a clean child suite does not inherit a false warning from its
  parent's prepared state.
- [ ] Prove one sibling suite's leak is attributed to that suite and becomes
  the next sibling's actual `S0`.
- [ ] Prove a persistent example leak can generate correctly scoped example
  and suite warnings.
- [ ] Prove a project lazy teardown that restores `S0` suppresses the suite
  warning.
- [ ] Prove mounts created during suite setup or teardown do not count as
  base-game screen changes and are gone before `S2`.
- [ ] Prove repeat boundaries compare and release independent `S0/S1/S2`
  records.
- [ ] Prove suite reset or settlement failure skips `S2`, preserves the cleanup
  error, and releases private identity tokens.

### Phase 4 gate

- [ ] Focused nested-suite lifecycle tests pass.
- [ ] Existing run completion and cleanup verification tests pass.
- [ ] Full unit suite passes.
- [ ] Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.

## Phase 5: Publish and render detached diagnostics

- [ ] Continue using `EventType.DIAGNOSTIC_RECORDED`; do not add a separate
  warning event type.
- [ ] Extend current event validation directly for the new diagnostic content;
  no backward-compatibility branch or schema migration is required.
- [ ] Validate scope, owner, comparison statuses, completeness, and detached
  observations.
- [ ] Append exactly one retained `WARNING ...` line for each change
  diagnostic.
- [ ] Format warning-severity diagnostics in `src/dwarfspec/report.lua`.
- [ ] Keep incomplete-verification diagnostics nonfatal and distinguish them
  from affirmative change warnings.
- [ ] Ensure only detached records reach the event journal and result files.
- [ ] Keep diagnostics out of `failure_details` and all test counters.

### Phase 5 tests

- [ ] Extend `tests/unit/automation_events_spec.lua` for valid and invalid
  example and suite diagnostic records.
- [ ] Extend `tests/unit/automation_host_spec.lua` for exactly-once event and
  retained-log publication.
- [ ] Extend `tests/unit/process_report_spec.lua` for post-result example
  warning formatting and suite warning formatting.
- [ ] Extend result, schema, runner, service, and immutable-enum fixtures only
  where their exhaustive inventories require the new data.
- [ ] Prove no event or JSON result contains userdata or an address-bearing
  screen label.
- [ ] Prove incomplete focus details remain attached to an affirmative screen
  change warning.
- [ ] Prove diagnostics do not affect terminal state, exit status, cleanup
  confirmation, or scheduler recovery.

### Phase 5 gate

- [ ] Focused event, report, host, result, and schema tests pass.
- [ ] Full unit suite passes.
- [ ] Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.

## Phase 6: Prove behavior in live DFHack

- [ ] Add
  `tests/automation/base_screen_focus_pollution_live_spec.lua`.
- [ ] Capture an outer fixture baseline and restore it in outer suite teardown
  so deliberate pollution never survives the focused run.
- [ ] Use deterministic suite and example names for warning-producer and
  observer ordering.
- [ ] Use a following observer example or sibling observer suite to inspect
  diagnostics emitted after the producer's body has completed.
- [ ] Prove an unchanged example emits no warning.
- [ ] Prove a per-example base-game navigation leak emits exactly one example
  warning after its success result.
- [ ] Prove restoration in project `after_each` suppresses the example warning.
- [ ] Prove all explicit mount categories are cleaned before example
  comparison and do not warn merely because they existed.
- [ ] Prove input through a native attachment warns when it leaves the
  underlying base-game focus changed.
- [ ] Prove suite lazy setup may establish a different `S1` without warning
  when lazy teardown restores `S0`.
- [ ] Prove an unrestored suite setup state emits exactly one suite warning
  containing `S0`, `S1`, and `S2`.
- [ ] Prove nested child and sibling suites receive correct inherited
  baselines and attribution.
- [ ] Prove an affirmative screen change with unavailable focus data remains a
  change warning with incomplete details.
- [ ] Prove deliberate example and suite warnings leave the run passed with
  `cleanup_confirmed=true`.
- [ ] Prove the outer fixture's original base screen and focus are restored
  after all producer and observer suites complete.

### Phase 6 gate

- [ ] Focused live run passes with the exact expected diagnostic kinds, scopes,
  owners, counts, and ordering.
- [ ] Focused live run reports `cleanup_confirmed=true`.
- [ ] Full live automation suite passes.
- [ ] Audit the full run's event journal against the exact deliberate-warning
  inventory; a zero exit status alone is insufficient.
- [ ] Full unit suite passes.
- [ ] Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.
- [ ] `git diff --check` passes.

## Phase 7: Document and close the feature

- [ ] Document example and suite pollution warnings in
  `docs/writing-tests.md`.
- [ ] Document the `S0/S1/S2` and `T0/T1` lifecycle boundaries.
- [ ] Explain that suite setup may change prepared state but suite teardown is
  expected to restore inherited state.
- [ ] Explain mounted-component exclusions, warning timing, incomplete details,
  and nonfatal behavior.
- [ ] Document retained warning output and unchanged process-exit semantics in
  `docs/command-line.md`.
- [ ] Add the feature under `Unreleased` in `CHANGELOG.md`.
- [ ] Mark the linked Testing Features item in `docs/project.todo` complete
  only after every implementation and verification gate above is satisfied.
- [ ] Record focused unit, full unit, focused live, full live, event-journal,
  syntax, formatting, process-result, and cleanup evidence below.

